  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1153 PM CDT SUN JUL 26 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery this evening paints a fairly active picture\nacross the country with an upper trough moving across the eastern\nCONUS and a stout upper ridge out west. In between these two\nfeatures, a shortwave is moving into the Tennessee Valley. As of\nwriting, this shortwave is currently guiding a southward moving\nMCS into northern Alabama. When timing out the ongoing MCS, it \nshould be on our doorstep around 2 AM. The RRFS seems to have a \nbetter handle on the timing. Meanwhile, the recent HRRR hints at \nthis cluster fizzling out with additional activity developing in \nthe mid morning hours. We will be keeping an eye on the trends \nover the next several hours. Scattered showers and storms will be \nlikely through the early afternoon hours Monday as activity \nredevelops along lingering boundaries. The main threats tonight \nthrough Monday afternoon will be strong winds and heavy rainfall. \nLatest CAMs place the heaviest rainfall to the east of I-65 with a\nfew pockets of 2-3\" through the morning hours. This would lead to\nsome localized flash flooding.\n\nUpper level ridging begins to shift east as we head through the\nwork week. Several bouts of H5 energy will rotate along the\neastern periphery of the ridge, leading to additional rounds of\nshowers and storms late Tuesday into Wednesday. The main story \nfor the week will be our increasing temperatures. High temps on \nMonday look to climb into the low to mid 90s, further increasing \ninto the upper 90s to around 100 on Tuesday. Dew points in the mid\nto upper 70s will lead to major heat risk for the majority of \nCentral Alabama. Went ahead and issued a Heat Advisory for Monday \nafternoon for the western half of the area. The increased speed of\nthe MCS should allow us to warm up efficiently through the day \nMonday. With that said, the second round of convection during the \nday may lead to some cooler conditions for those lucky enough to \ncatch a shower or storm. Conditions outside of any convection will\nbe hot and humid. A Heat Advisory is already in effect for \nTuesday as it looks to be our hottest and most uncomfortable day \nof the week with heat indices up around 110 for some. Therefore, \nopted to run Monday's Advisory through the night until it expands \nTuesday morning. By mid-week, the ridge appears to become slightly\nsuppressed as a trough builds over the East Coast. This pattern \nchange will allow for slightly cooler temperatures to filter in \nfrom the north, with highs trending toward the upper- 80s and \nlow-90s by next Friday.\n\n95/Castillo",
